  "chapter": 749,
  "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
  "text": " \n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 112 Chapter 749\n \n       MEMORANDUM filed with Assembly Bill Number 7272, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the private housing finance law, in relation to\n        voting, election  and  referendum  procedures;  to  requirements\n        regarding   mutual  housing  companies  considering  dissolution\n        and/or reconstitution; to certain duties of a board of directors\n        of a limited-profit housing company; and to prohibiting  certain\n        limited-profit  housing  companies  from  voluntarily dissolving\n        during the state disaster emergency declared in response to  the\n        outbreak of COVID-19\"\n \n    APPROVED\n \n  This bill makes a variety of changes to the governance of mutual hous-\ning  companies. The bill creates a new section 13-c in the Private Hous-\ning Finance Law requiring secret  ballots  to  be  used  in  shareholder\nelections,  creates  procedures  for  absentee  voting,  prohibits proxy\nvoting for most issues, requires a record be created for the purposes of\nelection audits, sets eligibility standards for co-op board  candidates,\nand defines dwelling units for the purposes of vote counting.\n \n  The  bill  also  creates  a  new  section  35-a of the Private Housing\nFinance Law, which requires an eighty percent vote for any step  of  the\ndissolution process, prohibits operating funds to be used to pay for any\nstep  of  the  dissolution process, and prevents a dissolution vote from\nbeing called within five years of a prior dissolution vote failing.\n \n  Section 3 of the bill amends section 17 of the Private Housing Finance\nLaw to require that the boards of the mutual housing companies hold  six\nmeetings  per  year  with  the shareholders, file with their supervising\nagency a record of all of the votes from the board meetings, give notice\nof, and make available to shareholders any  communications  between  the\nmutual housing company and its supervising agency about specific issues,\nand  investigate any substantive allegation that a tenant is not occupy-\ning their apartment as their  primary  residence.  The  section  further\nprohibits  housing  companies from interfering with tenants who organize\ninto a tenants' association, and requires that housing  companies  allow\ntenants'  associations  to  meet in a public space without paying a fee.\nFinally, the amended section 17 requires that the board take all  neces-\nsary  and  appropriate  actions  to  ensure  that the management company\ncomplies with the requirements of subdivision 5 of section 17.\n \n  Section 4 of the bill prohibits companies or urban  rental  companies,\nas  defined  in  section  12  of  the  private  housing finance law from\ndissolving or undergoing semi-privatization,  or  taking  steps  towards\nthose  objectives  during the state disaster emergency declared pursuant\nto executive order 202 of 2020 in response  to  the  outbreak  of  novel\ncoronavirus, COVID-19.\n \n  I  am  supportive of this bill, however, there are a number of techni-\ncal, operational and policy issues that have led me to negotiate  neces-\nsary changes with the Legislature. The changes include setting forth the\nspecific votes that require secret ballots, clarifying the procedure for\nabsentee voting, listing the votes for which proxy voting is prohibited,\nclarifying vote audit records can be either paper or electronic, setting\nthe  required  number  of board meetings at four, permitting supervising\n \nagencies to promulgate  regulations  to  create  co-op  board  candidate\neligibility  requirements.  Provided  that  arrearages  of less than two\nmonths  maintenance  cannot  be  disqualifying,  provides  that  records\nregarding  board votes are maintained and posted on a website maintained\nby the mutual housing company and not filed with and maintained  by  the\nDivision  of  Housing  and  Community  Renewal.  Further clarifying, the\nchanges provided for language to ensure  that  confidential  information\nremains  protected,  clarifying language on the number of votes required\nfor  dissolution  or  conversion,  and  clarifying  language  that   the\nprovisions  in  the bill are prospective only. Finally, the amended bill\nincludes a standard severability clause.\n \n  With these changes, I am pleased to sign  this  piece  of  legislation\ninto law.\n \n  This bill is approved.                         (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                                ______\n",
  "year": 2021
 },
